`satd/tests/e2e/webhooks.rs:820` fails intermittently in CI:

```
assertion `left == right` failed: the missed span was re-sent; a best-effort
hook resumes at the head and does not carry history across a reload
```

Seen once on a PR that touches nothing this test exercises, and green on the re-run of the same job.

## The race

The test counts retry attempts for the missed span, reloads, waits, and counts again:

```rust
let before = receiver.all_attempts().await.iter().filter(|r| missed(r)).count();

// Retire the generation with a same-URL alertfile edit …
write_alertfile(&dir, &receiver, "chain", "\"chain\"", true);
crate::streaming::sighup_with_conf(&sn, "").await;
tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(3)).await;

let after = receiver.all_attempts().await.iter().filter(|r| missed(r)).count();
assert_eq!(after, before, …);
```

A retry that was **already in flight** when `before` was taken lands after it, so `after > before` without any history having been carried across the reload — which is the property the test exists to check. The dispatcher is retrying the outage span on its own schedule; nothing synchronises that against the snapshot.

## A fix that keeps the property

Take the baseline *after* the reload has settled, then assert it does not grow:

```rust
crate::streaming::sighup_with_conf(&sn, "").await;
tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(3)).await;      // in-flight retries land
let settled = receiver.all_attempts().await.iter().filter(|r| missed(r)).count();
tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_secs(3)).await;
let later = receiver.all_attempts().await.iter().filter(|r| missed(r)).count();
assert_eq!(later, settled, …);
```

A generation that really did carry its queue across the reload keeps retrying, so the count keeps growing and the test still fails — which is the regression worth catching. The `before > 0` assertion earlier in the test already establishes that the outage span was attempted at all.

