## Summary

`infer_owner` in `scripts/ai/analyze_agent_tooling.py` returned "Repository Integration" whenever a rule matched no `OWNER_KEYWORDS` entry. A caller couldn't tell an inferred owner from a defaulted one. 15 skill directories have no keyword, so a rule pointing at any of them would silently report an owner nobody determined. Closes #321.

- **Stand-alone rules** (no equivalent skill) still get the default, as the code comment intends. Today that is only `analysis-artifacts.mdc`.
- **A rule mapped to a skill with no keyword match** now gets an explicit `Unassigned (no OWNER_KEYWORDS entry)` owner.
- **A new baseline check, `rule owners`,** fails on an unassigned owner and names the rule and its skill. It also fails on:
  - a rule file that isn't valid UTF-8;
  - a recommended rule whose inferred owner differs from the one it declares.

  Specific `revenue-cloud-docs` and `document-generation` keywords now come before the generic `doc`. The miss now surfaces at PR time through `analyze_agent_tooling.py check`, which the gate runs on every PR.

None of the 12 current rules hits the new path, so the generated report doesn't change.

## Verification

- `analyze_agent_tooling.py check`: PASS, 0 failing of 14 checks. `rule owners` reports `12 rule owners assigned: 11 inferred, 1 stand-alone defaulted to Repository Integration`. A failure to collect rules now fails the check instead of skipping it (`e95618de`).
- `tests/test_agent_launch_checks.py`: all pass. 10 `RuleOwners` tests. They cover:
  - a keyword match and specific doc owners beating the generic `doc` keyword;
  - the stand-alone default;
  - the unmapped case and its failing check;
  - the success detail;
  - a rule-collection error and an invalid-UTF-8 rule file both failing;
  - an inference that contradicts a recommendation;
  - the repo's current rules.
- `pr_gate.py --base origin/main`: all selected checks pass.

